"A plain stick, however, is not a very good clock. As you saw in Lesson 3, the Sun takes a high path in summer and a low path in winter, so the shadow at nine o'clock in June does not land where it lands at nine o'clock in December. The fix is to tilt the stick so that it points at the North Star, parallel to Earth's axis, as you saw in Lesson 6. In Changhua, that means tilting it 24 degrees above the northern horizon. Now the shadow turns evenly, 15 degrees every hour, all year round. This tilted pointer is called the gnomon, and the great equatorial sundial in front of the Hall of Supreme Harmony in Beijing's Forbidden City works exactly this way.": "f23f19b28b00",
"Give two reasons a Changhua sundial and a clock can disagree.": "833aede6748e",
"If you check a sundial against your watch, you will notice they rarely agree. A clock ticks at the same rate all year, but the Sun does not keep perfect time. Earth's orbit is an oval, so Earth moves faster in January, when it is closest to the Sun, and its tilted axis adds another wobble. Together, they make sundial time run up to about 16 minutes ahead of clock time in early November and about 14 minutes behind in mid-February. Astronomers call this difference the equation of time.": "e49ef3653c12",
"Long before anyone owned a clock, the Sun was the clock. Push a stick straight into the ground on a sunny morning, and its shadow stretches far to the west. As the hours pass, the shadow swings around and shrinks, until around midday it is at its shortest and points due north. Then it grows again toward the east until sunset. An old Chinese saying, \"set up a pole and see its shadow,\" still means getting results right away. With a stick and a few pebbles to mark the shadow, anyone can tell the time.": "d3679609eabd",
"Long before anyone owned a clock, the Sun was the clock. Push a stick straight into the ground on a sunny morning, and its shadow stretches far to the west. As the hours pass, the shadow swings around and shrinks, until around midday it is at its shortest and points due north. Then it grows again toward the east until sunset. An old Chinese saying, \"set up a pole and see its shadow,\" still means getting results right away. With a stick and a few pebbles to mark the shadow, anyone can tell the time. The shadow moves because Earth spins. Every hour, Earth turns 15 degrees, so the Sun appears to cross the sky from east to west, and the shadow always points away from the Sun. In the morning, when the Sun is low in the east, the shadow is long and points west. At noon, the Sun is highest in the south, and the shadow is shortest. In the evening, the Sun sinks in the west, and the shadow stretches long toward the east. A plain stick, however, is not a very good clock. As you saw in Lesson 3, the Sun takes a high path in summer and a low path in winter, so the shadow at nine o'clock in June does not land where it lands at nine o'clock in December. The fix is to tilt the stick so that it points at the North Star, parallel to Earth's axis, as you saw in Lesson 6. In Changhua, that means tilting it 24 degrees above the northern horizon. Now the shadow turns evenly, 15 degrees every hour, all year round. This tilted pointer is called the gnomon, and the great equatorial sundial in front of the Hall of Supreme Harmony in Beijing's Forbidden City works exactly this way. The shadow's length is a calendar, too. At noon, the shadow is shortest at the summer solstice and longest at the winter solstice. Chinese astronomers measured it for thousands of years with a gnomon called a guibiao: a tall pole with a ruler lying north along the ground. In 1276, Guo Shoujing built one 40 chi tall, five times the usual height, and measured the length of the year as 365.2425 days, the same value our calendar uses today. In Changhua, the noon shadow almost disappears in June, because the Tropic of Cancer passes just to the south, through Chiayi. If you check a sundial against your watch, you will notice they rarely agree. A clock ticks at the same rate all year, but the Sun does not keep perfect time. Earth's orbit is an oval, so Earth moves faster in January, when it is closest to the Sun, and its tilted axis adds another wobble. Together, they make sundial time run up to about 16 minutes ahead of clock time in early November and about 14 minutes behind in mid-February. Astronomers call this difference the equation of time. There is one more twist. All of Taiwan sets its clocks by the Sun's position at 120 degrees east, but Changhua lies a little farther east, so its sundials run about 2 minutes ahead. Add it all up, and a Changhua sundial is about 19 minutes fast in early November and about 12 minutes slow in mid-February. Before time zones, every town set its clocks by its own Sun. Railroads in the United States finally adopted standard time zones in 1883, so trains could keep one schedule. On the next sunny day, stand a pencil in the ground and see what time the Sun says it is.": "f79c6480c602",
